  │ [38;5;153mconsole.error("Validation error:", …)[39m │ [38;5;153mlog.warn("validation_failed", { reason: validation.error })[39m — a fixed   │
  │  ([38;5;153m:26[39m)                                │ string, not user input                                                  │
  └───────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Plus two additions: the submit handler gets a [38;5;153mtry/catch[39m that routes throws to [38;5;153mlog.error(err, { op: "submit" })[39m, and
  [38;5;153mlogger.js[39m installs global [38;5;153merror[39m / [38;5;153munhandledrejection[39m handlers so failures outside the handler aren't invisible. When
  the real [38;5;153mfetch[39m to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m replaces the stub, Sentry's network breadcrumbs record method, URL, and status — never
  request bodies — so the eventual login POST won't carry credentials into the vendor.

  One open question for this section.

  The fork is [1mwhether the vendor learns which account hit the error.[0m With user context set, a production report reads
  "login failed for [38;5;153malice@corp[39m" and you can check that one account; without it, you get "a login failed somewhere" and
  have to reproduce blind. The cost is that usernames — likely email addresses — become personal data held by Sentry,
  subject to whatever retention and deletion obligations you have. This is cheap to turn on later and expensive to turn
  off later, since data already sent has to be purged rather than just stopped.

  I recommend the pseudonymous middle option: hash the username client-side and send the digest. Two reports from the
  same account visibly match, which is most of the diagnostic value, but the vendor never holds an identifier you'd have
  to purge.
